{
  "title": "Body and health",
  "title_target": "Gorputza eta osasuna",
  "goal": "Name parts of the body, say what hurts, and get by at the pharmacy or the doctor's office.",
  "bekal": [
    "name the body parts people talk about most",
    "use the pattern *…ko mina dut* for any kind of pain",
    "describe symptoms and understand how to take medicine"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Elkarrizketa — Farmazian",
      "intro": "Budi walks into the pharmacy on the corner with a hoarse voice and a tired face.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Farmazialaria:",
          "text": "Egun on. Zer moduz zaude?",
          "id": "Good morning. How are you feeling?",
          "literal": "day good. what way-of you-are?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Ez oso ondo. Buruko mina dut.",
          "id": "Not great. I have a headache.",
          "literal": "not very well. head-of pain-the I-have."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Farmazialaria:",
          "text": "Sukarrik baduzu?",
          "id": "Do you have a fever?",
          "literal": "fever-any you-have?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Uste dut baietz. Eta eztarriko mina ere bai.",
          "id": "I think so. And a sore throat too.",
          "literal": "think I-have yes-that. and throat-of pain-the also yes."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Farmazialaria:",
          "text": "Noiztik zaude horrela?",
          "id": "How long have you been like this?",
          "literal": "when-from you-are like-that?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Herenegundik. Gaur okerrago nago.",
          "id": "Since the day before yesterday. Today I'm worse.",
          "literal": "day-before-yesterday-from. today bad-er I-am."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Farmazialaria:",
          "text": "Katarroa duzu, ziur asko. Hartu pastilla hauek.",
          "id": "You've got a cold, most likely. Take these tablets.",
          "literal": "cold-the you-have, sure much. take tablet these."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Zenbat hartu behar ditut?",
          "id": "How many do I have to take?",
          "literal": "how-many take need I-have-them?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Farmazialaria:",
          "text": "Bat egunean hirutan, bazkalostean.",
          "id": "One, three times a day, after lunch.",
          "literal": "one day-in three-times, lunch-after-at."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Ados. Eta eztarrirako zerbait?",
          "id": "OK. And something for the throat?",
          "literal": "agreed. and throat-for something?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Farmazialaria:",
          "text": "Xarabe hau ona da. Eta ur asko edan, e!",
          "id": "This syrup is good. And drink lots of water, OK!",
          "literal": "syrup this good-the is. and water much drink, eh!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Eskerrik asko. Osasuna lehenengo!",
          "id": "Thank you. Health comes first!",
          "literal": "thanks many. health-the first!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Buruko mina dut — one pattern for every ache.** The formula: body part + *-ko* + *mina dut*. *Buru**ko** mina* (headache), *sabel**eko** mina* (stomachache), *hortz**etako** mina* (toothache), *bizkar**reko** mina* (backache). This one pattern replaces dozens of sentences.",
        "**Uste dut baietz — \"I think so\".** *Baietz* is a special \"yes\" used after verbs of thinking; its partner is *ezetz* (\"no\"). *Uste dut baietz* (I think so), *uste dut ezetz* (I don't think so). Two ready-made sentences that rescue many conversations.",
        "**Noiztik — since when.** The ending *-tik* you learned for places (\"from Bilbo\") also works for time: *herenegun**dik*** (since the day before yesterday), *atzo**tik*** (since yesterday), *goize**tik*** (since this morning). Its partner is *noiz arte* (\"until when\").",
        "**Egunean hirutan — how many times a day.** The pattern: the time unit with *-an*, then the number of times with *-tan*. *Egunean bitan* (twice a day), *astean behin* (once a week), *hilean bitan* (twice a month). Note that \"once\" is irregular: *behin*, not *batetan*."
      ]
    }
  ],
